WEBVTT
Language: en

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:05.000
Welcome to Mosaic.

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:07.360
In this video, we'll talk about
how the operating system influences

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:08.960
the performance of the processor.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.780
Before we begin,
let's clarify some basic concepts.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:15.460
Operating systems are a set of
programs in charge of managing

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:17.700
a computer's resources,

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:19.460
such as memory, the hard drive,

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:20.880
the processor, among others.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:23.520
The processor is the brain of the system.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:27.080
It processes everything that happens in
the computer and carries out every action.

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:30.360
A process is a program that is running.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:32.980
Processes can be in different states,

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:35.860
among them the unborn, or new, state,

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:37.580
which is when a process exists,

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:39.220
but the CPU doesn't know about it yet,

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:42.740
either because it is very recent or
because it hasn't been asked to run yet.

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:46.280
Processes in the active,
or running, state are the processes

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:48.500
the processor is executing at that moment.

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:52.460
A process in the ready state is
a process that is ready to run

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:53.520
at any moment.

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:56.460
A process in the blocked state
is a process that is waiting

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:58.820
for a response from some other process,

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:00.780
so it can continue running.

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:05.460
And a finished process is one that
has already completed its execution.

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:07.920
Maybe you don't fully get it yet,

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:10.720
but don't worry, we'll go
through a small example.

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:13.900
In an ATM that is
validating the account data,

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:17.800
the processes that carry out this task
are the processes in the active state.

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:19.600
Once the data has been validated,

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:22.420
these processes move
to the finished state.

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:24.800
Then the customer can
choose what they need to do,

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:26.940
so the processes to make a deposit,

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:28.900
withdraw money, or check
the account statement,

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:29.940
are in the ready state.

00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:31.980
However,

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:35.700
the process that accesses the person's
information is in the blocked state,

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:39.620
waiting to know what data the chosen
process will need, so it can request it

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:40.780
from the database.

00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:43.810
Once the person has
completed their transaction,

00:01:43.910 --> 00:01:47.190
the processes will move to the
finished state until they are

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:48.190
requested again.

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:51.770
Now we know all the different
states of a process,

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:52.990
but how does the processor know

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:55.590
when to run those processes?

00:01:56.050 --> 00:01:58.770
This is where processor
scheduling comes in,

00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:02.230
which is when it's decided how much
execution time is assigned to each process

00:02:02.230 --> 00:02:04.890
in the system, and when it will run.

00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:07.950
Let's say it's like the
processor's schedule.

00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:12.490
Its job is to divide the
processor's available time among all

00:02:12.490 --> 00:02:13.710
the processes.

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:16.650
Schedulers can be of three types,

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:19.610
each type with a specific goal.

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:22.670
There are long-term, or job, schedulers.

00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:25.570
They are in charge of scheduling
processes that will last a long time,

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:28.330
and therefore,
they spread them out over time.

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:32.650
These can be some system processes that
will be in constant use all the time,

00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:34.830
but are not always running,

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:37.460
only at specific moments.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:41.460
The goal is not to overload the processor
with the system's constant processes

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:44.020
but to pace them over time.

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:45.140
Then there are medium-term
schedulers, or swappers.

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:50.720
They swap out the non-essential
processes to be run later

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:52.760
and prioritize the essential processes.

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:55.780
You can see this, for example,
when the computer boots up.

00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:58.920
The processes not essential for
booting are placed on the disk

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:01.320
and the essential ones in main memory,

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:05.580
but once booting finishes, the processes

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:08.960
that were waiting on the disk
are moved back to main memory.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:10.000
This exchange process

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:15.180
is called swapping. And finally,
the short-term schedulers, or dispatchers;

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:19.780
their main function is to set up the
processes that are needed in real

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:20.560
time.

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:23.760
This is used in ATM
systems to achieve faster

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:28.680
execution, or in the applications
in use, such as the browser.

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:31.440
When processes are
assigned to the processor,

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:35.360
two strategies are used that
define how the process will run.

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:39.340
The non-preemptive strategy is
when the operating system cannot

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:42.520
take the CPU away from
a process that is running.

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:44.580
That is, once the process is running,

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:47.560
the operating system can't
stop it to run something else.

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:50.160
On the other hand,
there is the preemptive strategy,

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:53.260
which is when the operating system
can take the CPU away from a process

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:56.620
that is running and use
it to run another process.

00:03:57.380 --> 00:04:01.140
That's the main thing you need
to know about how processes take

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:05.480
place in the processor, organized
and defined by the operating system.

00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:06.880
So now that you know it,

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:11.560
it's your turn to think where process
management is useful in your daily life,

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:13.760
maybe when watching
a video or taking a class.

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:17.480
A person's attention is like the
use of the processor in a computer:

00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:20.240
limited, but very powerful if used well.
